1
00:00:01,210 --> 00:00:03,990
Hello everyone, this is
Dr. Carl Abraham from the

2
00:00:03,990 --> 00:00:07,220
Department of Clinical
Medicine at NYIT College of

3
00:00:07,220 --> 00:00:10,750
Osteopathic Medicine here to
present to you a session on

4
00:00:10,750 --> 00:00:14,090
nosocomial infections
including nosocomial pneumonia.

5
00:00:14,910 --> 00:00:18,250
During this lecture, we
will define nosocomial

6
00:00:18,250 --> 00:00:20,950
infections and talk
about their prevalence,

7
00:00:21,310 --> 00:00:25,230
what the most common
etiologic agents are,

8
00:00:25,230 --> 00:00:29,150
and explain the importance
of infection control

9
00:00:29,150 --> 00:00:32,830
and major causes of
nosocomial infections.

10
00:00:33,070 --> 00:00:35,970
We'll describe what
infection control programs

11
00:00:35,970 --> 00:00:42,570
do and try to appreciate
the interventions that

12
00:00:42,570 --> 00:00:45,170
are put in place, such as
isolation and precautions,

13
00:00:45,170 --> 00:00:50,050
so that nosocomial
infections are decreased.

14
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:56,110
Nosocomial infections
are infections that

15
00:00:56,110 --> 00:00:59,410
occur in the hospitalized
patients that are

16
00:00:59,410 --> 00:01:02,550
not present upon
admission to the hospital.

17
00:01:02,970 --> 00:01:05,850
Importantly, nosocomial
infections are considered

18
00:01:05,850 --> 00:01:09,090
to be entirely
preventable. Even so,

19
00:01:09,090 --> 00:01:12,290
every year nosocomial
infections occur in millions

20
00:01:12,290 --> 00:01:14,730
of hospitalized
patients across the U.S.

21
00:01:14,970 --> 00:01:19,230
at a significant cost to
those patients and to the

22
00:01:19,230 --> 00:01:22,590
health care system in
general to the tune of

23
00:01:22,590 --> 00:01:27,570
somewhere between $28 and
$45 billion a year in 2010.

24
00:01:31,150 --> 00:01:33,970
Although there
can be nosocomial

25
00:01:33,970 --> 00:01:36,490
outbreaks of influenza
and other viruses,

26
00:01:36,490 --> 00:01:38,410
the vast majority of nosocomial

27
00:01:38,410 --> 00:01:40,350
infections are
caused by bacteria.

28
00:01:40,370 --> 00:01:44,350
Since these infections occur
in hospitals, where the

29
00:01:44,350 --> 00:01:47,010
number of persons receiving
antibiotics is highest,

30
00:01:47,290 --> 00:01:49,730
many nosocomial
infections are caused by

31
00:01:49,730 --> 00:01:52,210
bacteria that are
multidrug-resistant.

32
00:01:52,690 --> 00:01:55,170
Antibiotic resistance
is associated with

33
00:01:55,170 --> 00:01:58,750
increased cost and
morbidity and mortality.

34
00:01:59,010 --> 00:02:01,770
In addition, the antibiotics
that are necessary

35
00:02:01,770 --> 00:02:05,670
for many of these organisms
are necessarily very

36
00:02:05,670 --> 00:02:08,730
broad spectrum, thus
increasing the amount of

37
00:02:08,730 --> 00:02:11,170
drug resistance within
the hospital even further.

38
00:02:11,550 --> 00:02:14,270
Unfortunately, there are
nosocomial infections

39
00:02:14,270 --> 00:02:16,450
caused by bacteria
that are resistant to

40
00:02:16,450 --> 00:02:19,790
all antibiotic therapy,
severely limiting

41
00:02:19,790 --> 00:02:22,170
the therapeutic options
for these patients.

42
00:02:24,550 --> 00:02:27,910
And in 1999, the
Institute of Medicine

43
00:02:27,910 --> 00:02:31,230
released the seminal
report to Air is Human,

44
00:02:31,230 --> 00:02:36,050
documenting that approximately
100,000 lives were

45
00:02:36,050 --> 00:02:38,910
lost to hospital errors
in the United States

46
00:02:38,910 --> 00:02:42,830
every year, the majority
of which were preventable

47
00:02:42,830 --> 00:02:46,070
hospital-acquired
infections, making this the

48
00:02:46,070 --> 00:02:49,250
fourth leading cause of
death in the United States.

49
00:02:50,610 --> 00:02:53,710
The most common
nosocomial infections are

50
00:02:53,710 --> 00:02:56,150
associated with
insertable medical devices

51
00:02:56,150 --> 00:02:58,830
such as central venous
and urinary catheters,

52
00:02:59,270 --> 00:03:01,770
endotracheal intubation, and

53
00:03:01,770 --> 00:03:04,170
also surgical site infections.

54
00:03:04,430 --> 00:03:09,050
The common denominator
of these infections is

55
00:03:09,050 --> 00:03:13,190
breaching host immune
defenses. And in the case of

56
00:03:13,190 --> 00:03:15,750
the first three of
these, the presence of a

57
00:03:15,750 --> 00:03:18,430
foreign body that has no
blood supply or cellular

58
00:03:18,430 --> 00:03:21,630
immunity that can ward
off bacterial pathogens.

59
00:03:22,770 --> 00:03:26,270
Clostridium difficile
infection is a colitis that

60
00:03:26,270 --> 00:03:29,410
is an adverse effect of
antibiotic therapy that can

61
00:03:29,410 --> 00:03:32,750
be prolonged and in some
cases life-threatening.

62
00:03:35,570 --> 00:03:38,970
The field of hospital
medicine that deals with

63
00:03:38,970 --> 00:03:41,750
nosocomial infections is
called infection control.

64
00:03:42,040 --> 00:03:44,850
In most healthcare
facilities, such as hospitals

65
00:03:44,850 --> 00:03:47,530
and nursing homes, there
are departments and

66
00:03:47,530 --> 00:03:50,410
practitioners of infection
control whose responsibility

67
00:03:50,410 --> 00:03:53,010
is to intervene and
prevent nosocomial

68
00:03:53,010 --> 00:03:56,670
infections, which are, and
this is very important,

69
00:03:56,870 --> 00:03:59,770
considered to be
entirely preventable.

70
00:04:02,450 --> 00:04:04,930
After the release of the
Institute of Medicine's

71
00:04:04,930 --> 00:04:06,930
report, departments
of infection control

72
00:04:06,930 --> 00:04:10,370
were put on notice to
decrease infections or

73
00:04:10,370 --> 00:04:13,010
hospitals would suffer
economic consequences.

74
00:04:13,180 --> 00:04:16,170
And you can see that from
the millions of infections

75
00:04:16,170 --> 00:04:19,130
that were occurring in
the 1990s, there was

76
00:04:19,130 --> 00:04:22,450
actually much success to
decrease that number so

77
00:04:22,450 --> 00:04:25,550
that by the 20-teens, we
were in the hundreds of

78
00:04:25,550 --> 00:04:29,150
thousands, although that
is still far too many.

79
00:04:31,530 --> 00:04:34,590
Infection control began
in the 19th century when

80
00:04:34,590 --> 00:04:38,270
Ignat Semmelweis recognized
that mothers died at

81
00:04:38,270 --> 00:04:41,750
drastically increased rates
when his students delivered

82
00:04:41,750 --> 00:04:45,390
as opposed to midwives.
He surmised that his

83
00:04:45,390 --> 00:04:49,050
students contaminated their
hands with something during

84
00:04:49,050 --> 00:04:51,730
autopsies that were being
done just prior to the

85
00:04:51,730 --> 00:04:55,210
deliveries, and the midwives
did not perform these

86
00:04:55,210 --> 00:04:59,190
autopsies. After he
instituted hand-washing rules.

87
00:05:00,150 --> 00:05:03,630
Postpartum mortality
decreased dramatically,

88
00:05:03,630 --> 00:05:06,910
and the field of infection
control was born.

89
00:05:09,110 --> 00:05:11,890
And it turns out that
hand-washing is the most

90
00:05:11,890 --> 00:05:14,310
effective intervention
that health care workers

91
00:05:14,310 --> 00:05:17,370
can perform to decrease
nosocomial infections.

92
00:05:17,890 --> 00:05:21,030
Multidrug-resistant bacteria
are transferred from

93
00:05:21,030 --> 00:05:24,130
patient to patient by the
hands of health care workers.

94
00:05:24,250 --> 00:05:27,570
Although this slide lists
instances in which hand

95
00:05:27,570 --> 00:05:30,890
washing is important, it
is actually very important

96
00:05:30,890 --> 00:05:33,950
to perform some sort
of hand hygiene after

97
00:05:33,950 --> 00:05:38,350
each patient contact, as
well as contact with possibly

98
00:05:38,350 --> 00:05:42,190
infected surfaces such
as keyboards, doorknobs,

99
00:05:42,190 --> 00:05:45,310
elevator buttons, cell
phones, and even clothing.

100
00:05:45,310 --> 00:05:49,150
For example, the sleeves
of lab coats. These

101
00:05:49,150 --> 00:05:52,910
are all high-frequency
touch surfaces that may be

102
00:05:52,910 --> 00:05:55,990
contaminated by the hands
of healthcare workers.

103
00:05:57,930 --> 00:06:01,230
Alcohol-based hand
sanitizers are a good

104
00:06:01,230 --> 00:06:02,990
substitute for soap
and water because

105
00:06:02,990 --> 00:06:05,150
of the ease of use
and availability.

106
00:06:05,570 --> 00:06:08,910
Dispensers are located at
the doorways of every patient

107
00:06:08,910 --> 00:06:12,830
room and other high-frequency
touch areas. However,

108
00:06:12,830 --> 00:06:16,550
they are not appropriate
in all cases and cannot be

109
00:06:16,550 --> 00:06:19,950
used for hand hygiene when
there is visible blood or

110
00:06:19,950 --> 00:06:23,490
soiling or to disinfect
hands that are contaminated

111
00:06:23,490 --> 00:06:27,650
with spore-forming organisms,
such as Clostridium difficile.

112
00:06:27,710 --> 00:06:31,570
Soap and water must be
used in those instances.

113
00:06:34,720 --> 00:06:36,930
Interventions to
decrease the spread

114
00:06:36,930 --> 00:06:42,050
of nosocomial pathogens
include isolation

115
00:06:42,050 --> 00:06:45,150
and certain types
of precautions.

116
00:06:46,570 --> 00:06:49,630
Isolation, which we'll talk
about in detail in a few

117
00:06:49,630 --> 00:06:54,010
slides comes in two types. Type
1 prevents the transmission

118
00:06:54,010 --> 00:06:58,290
from the patient to healthcare
workers of organisms

119
00:06:58,290 --> 00:07:02,870
and thus prevents
contaminations of subsequent

120
00:07:02,870 --> 00:07:06,430
patients that are cared for
by that healthcare worker.

121
00:07:06,870 --> 00:07:10,970
Type 2 isolation prevents
the transmission from

122
00:07:10,970 --> 00:07:17,410
healthcare workers to patients
at risk of normal commensal

123
00:07:17,410 --> 00:07:21,070
bacteria, as well as
multidrug-resistant pathogens.

124
00:07:21,230 --> 00:07:26,970
And these are usually
performed in patients or for

125
00:07:26,970 --> 00:07:30,570
patients that are immune
suppressed, such as

126
00:07:30,570 --> 00:07:34,290
patients in the neonatal
intensive care unit, bone

127
00:07:34,290 --> 00:07:39,630
marrow or organ transplant
units, or in patients that

128
00:07:39,630 --> 00:07:47,590
have granulocytopenia
precautions can also be

129
00:07:47,590 --> 00:07:50,870
divided into standard
versus transmission based

130
00:07:51,190 --> 00:07:55,230
standard precautions are
what we do for every patient

131
00:07:55,490 --> 00:07:58,650
transmission based precautions
are interventions to

132
00:07:58,650 --> 00:08:00,750
decrease the transmission
of certain pathogens

133
00:08:00,750 --> 00:08:04,490
from patients to health
care workers and vice versa.

134
00:08:07,380 --> 00:08:10,470
Standard precautions,
again, what we do for every

135
00:08:10,470 --> 00:08:13,850
patient as a baseline,
include interventions such

136
00:08:13,850 --> 00:08:17,730
as frequent hand hygiene,
using certain personal

137
00:08:17,730 --> 00:08:21,530
protective equipment or
PPE for certain procedures

138
00:08:21,530 --> 00:08:24,090
such as wearing gloves
when cleaning a wound,

139
00:08:24,090 --> 00:08:28,210
wearing a face mask, sterile
gloves when accessing

140
00:08:28,210 --> 00:08:31,730
a dialysis catheter, and
other such procedures.

141
00:08:32,010 --> 00:08:37,530
Standard precautions also
includes cough etiquette

142
00:08:37,530 --> 00:08:40,630
and specific practices
with regard to injections

143
00:08:40,630 --> 00:08:43,830
and handling of contaminated
equipment and surfaces.

144
00:08:46,310 --> 00:08:50,600
Patients admitted to the
hospital with infections or

145
00:08:50,600 --> 00:08:54,790
pathogen colonization that
are known to be passed from

146
00:08:54,790 --> 00:08:59,250
healthcare workers to patients
by touch, for example,

147
00:08:59,610 --> 00:09:02,170
methicillin-resistant
staphylococcus aureus,

148
00:09:02,410 --> 00:09:06,050
vancomycin-resistant
enterococcus, and clostridium

149
00:09:06,050 --> 00:09:10,390
difficile are placed
in contact precautions.

150
00:09:10,530 --> 00:09:13,450
Contact precautions includes
possibly placing the

151
00:09:13,450 --> 00:09:16,470
patient into a single
room, sometimes with one-on

152
00:09:16,470 --> 00:09:19,990
-one nursing, and all persons
entering the room wear

153
00:09:19,990 --> 00:09:23,530
gowns, gloves, and sometimes
head and foot covers.

154
00:09:23,530 --> 00:09:27,190
Remember, all transmission
-based precautions are

155
00:09:27,190 --> 00:09:30,370
interventions that are
in addition to standard

156
00:09:30,370 --> 00:09:34,530
precautions, so hand hygiene
is still very important.

157
00:09:38,080 --> 00:09:40,910
Patients are placed into
droplet precautions when

158
00:09:40,910 --> 00:09:43,490
they are colonized or
infected with pathogens such

159
00:09:43,490 --> 00:09:47,010
as Bordetella pertussis
and Neisseria meningitidis

160
00:09:47,010 --> 00:09:51,050
or influenza that are spread
by respiratory droplets

161
00:09:51,050 --> 00:09:54,010
produced by the patient
coughing or sneezing.

162
00:09:54,010 --> 00:09:56,770
These droplets are large
enough to be affected by

163
00:09:56,770 --> 00:09:59,790
gravity so that they
eventually fall to the floor.

164
00:10:00,110 --> 00:10:03,770
Persons entering the room
of a patient in droplet

165
00:10:03,770 --> 00:10:07,750
precautions wear regular
medical or surgical masks.

166
00:10:10,650 --> 00:10:14,730
And lastly, patients are
placed in airborne precautions

167
00:10:14,730 --> 00:10:16,610
when they are infected
with pathogens that

168
00:10:16,610 --> 00:10:20,110
have the ability to be
aerosolized, thus remaining in

169
00:10:20,110 --> 00:10:23,750
the air for minutes or
hours. Pathogens in this

170
00:10:23,750 --> 00:10:26,910
group include tuberculosis,
measles, and varicella.

171
00:10:27,050 --> 00:10:30,310
Persons entering the room
wear a specialized mask

172
00:10:30,310 --> 00:10:33,350
called an N95 that
filters out ultra-small

173
00:10:33,350 --> 00:10:36,950
particles and air in the
room is exchanged at least

174
00:10:36,950 --> 00:10:40,410
every five minutes and
exhausted to the outside,

175
00:10:40,410 --> 00:10:43,330
resulting in a negative
pressure in the room

176
00:10:43,330 --> 00:10:47,210
so that air always
flows into the room from

177
00:10:47,210 --> 00:10:49,990
the hallway and never
in the other direction.

178
00:10:52,150 --> 00:10:55,290
This slide shows the
most common organisms

179
00:10:55,290 --> 00:10:58,470
causing nosocomial
infections in hospitals.

180
00:10:58,710 --> 00:11:01,950
We'll discuss many of these
in the following slides.

181
00:11:05,390 --> 00:11:08,430
Clostridium difficile is
a gram-positive obligate

182
00:11:08,430 --> 00:11:11,270
anaerobe that colonizes the
colon of the majority of infants,

183
00:11:11,270 --> 00:11:14,890
but only about 3% of adults.
It lives in a delicate

184
00:11:14,890 --> 00:11:18,290
balance with other colonic
flora, And when that balance

185
00:11:18,290 --> 00:11:22,910
is changed so that C.
difficile overgrows, some

186
00:11:22,910 --> 00:11:26,450
strains produce two toxins
that result in tissue necrosis

187
00:11:26,450 --> 00:11:30,170
and pseudomembrane formation
that covers the walls of

188
00:11:30,170 --> 00:11:33,090
the colon, as shown here in
the bottom right picture.

189
00:11:33,290 --> 00:11:35,590
Toxin production
not only results in

190
00:11:35,590 --> 00:11:38,610
secretory diarrhea, but
can be complicated by

191
00:11:38,610 --> 00:11:41,490
life-threatening
toxic megacolon that

192
00:11:41,490 --> 00:11:44,750
requires collect me
to cure in many cases.

193
00:11:48,340 --> 00:11:52,150
In the mid-2000s,
mutations present in the C.

194
00:11:52,150 --> 00:11:54,930
difficile genome produced
the BINAP strain that

195
00:11:54,930 --> 00:11:59,210
overexpressed toxin A
and B and produced newly

196
00:11:59,210 --> 00:12:02,610
described toxins, resulting
in a hypervirulent

197
00:12:02,610 --> 00:12:06,290
strain that appears to be
also more transmissible.

198
00:12:06,790 --> 00:12:09,930
At its emergence,
it overran several

199
00:12:09,930 --> 00:12:12,390
hospitals in
Canada and the U.S.

200
00:12:12,710 --> 00:12:16,570
Before this C. difficile
colitis was almost exclusively

201
00:12:16,570 --> 00:12:18,890
a disease of persons who
had been in the hospital

202
00:12:18,890 --> 00:12:22,350
or at least receiving
chronic health care and had

203
00:12:22,350 --> 00:12:25,810
received antibiotics. It wasn't
long after the appearance

204
00:12:25,810 --> 00:12:29,010
of this hypervirulent strain
that community acquired

205
00:12:29,010 --> 00:12:32,810
cases were described and
now these are commonplace.

206
00:12:36,740 --> 00:12:40,010
The diagnosis of C.
difficile colitis is made by

207
00:12:40,010 --> 00:12:43,190
antigen or polymerase
chain reaction testing of

208
00:12:43,190 --> 00:12:47,470
dural stools. Prior to the
availability of these tests,

209
00:12:47,470 --> 00:12:50,290
C. difficile could be
cultured for diagnosis,

210
00:12:50,310 --> 00:12:53,870
but culture requires
specific media and can take

211
00:12:53,870 --> 00:12:58,310
several days while both
antigen and PCR are rapid

212
00:12:58,310 --> 00:13:01,530
tests with the results
available in several hours.

213
00:13:01,550 --> 00:13:04,250
That is very important
since it is very

214
00:13:04,250 --> 00:13:06,750
important to place these
patients in contact

215
00:13:06,750 --> 00:13:10,370
isolation as soon as
C. difficile colitis is

216
00:13:10,370 --> 00:13:13,350
suspected to prevent spread
to healthcare workers

217
00:13:13,350 --> 00:13:16,450
and other patients and
to start treatment.

218
00:13:20,710 --> 00:13:22,790
After Clostridium difficile,

219
00:13:23,210 --> 00:13:24,850
Staphylococcus species cause

220
00:13:24,850 --> 00:13:26,890
the most nosocomial infections,

221
00:13:27,090 --> 00:13:29,870
mostly bloodstream,
ventilator-associated

222
00:13:29,870 --> 00:13:32,430
pneumonia, and surgical
site infections.

223
00:13:33,410 --> 00:13:36,490
Staphylococci are gram
-positive, catalase-positive

224
00:13:36,490 --> 00:13:40,750
organisms that result in
pyogenic infections. In other

225
00:13:40,750 --> 00:13:44,910
words, they are pus
-forming. At least 50% of

226
00:13:44,910 --> 00:13:48,290
Staphylococcus aureus infections
in hospitalized patients

227
00:13:48,290 --> 00:13:51,890
are resistant to methicillin,
and these are termed

228
00:13:51,890 --> 00:13:55,970
methicillin-resistant
Staphylococcus aureus, or MRSA.

229
00:13:56,930 --> 00:13:59,390
Identification of
hospital patients who are

230
00:13:59,390 --> 00:14:02,650
colonized or infected
with MRSA is important,

231
00:14:02,730 --> 00:14:06,270
since MRSA is another organism
that can be passed from

232
00:14:06,270 --> 00:14:08,870
patient to patient on
the hands of healthcare

233
00:14:08,870 --> 00:14:13,110
workers. These patients are
placed in contact isolation.

234
00:14:17,730 --> 00:14:20,470
Staphylococci are beta
-hemolytic on blood agar

235
00:14:20,470 --> 00:14:22,650
plates, resulting in
a ring of hemolysis

236
00:14:22,650 --> 00:14:26,030
around colonies, as shown
on this slide. The most

237
00:14:26,030 --> 00:14:28,230
common species of
staphylococci causing

238
00:14:28,230 --> 00:14:31,730
nosocomial infection are
aureus and epidermidis.

239
00:14:32,290 --> 00:14:35,030
Staphylococcus epidermidis
is a skin commensal

240
00:14:35,030 --> 00:14:37,850
in all humans, while
Staphylococcus aureus

241
00:14:37,850 --> 00:14:41,530
colonizes the Nenarius in
about 30% of the population

242
00:14:41,530 --> 00:14:44,590
at any one time. It
turns out that being

243
00:14:44,590 --> 00:14:47,410
colonized with Staphylococcus
aureus increases one's

244
00:14:47,410 --> 00:14:50,750
risk of nosocomial
infection with that strain

245
00:14:50,750 --> 00:14:53,240
of Staphylococcus aureus
after hospitalization.

246
00:14:53,710 --> 00:14:57,830
During the early 2000s,
mutations in community

247
00:14:57,830 --> 00:15:01,450
isolates of methicillin
-sensitive Staphylococcus aureus

248
00:15:01,450 --> 00:15:06,230
resulted in methicillin
resistance and increased

249
00:15:06,440 --> 00:15:10,750
transmissibility, causing
outbreaks of community

250
00:15:10,750 --> 00:15:13,770
-acquired MRSA throughout
the U.S. and the world.

251
00:15:17,890 --> 00:15:19,990
As we talked about before,

252
00:15:20,590 --> 00:15:22,970
Staphylococci are
catalase positive,

253
00:15:22,970 --> 00:15:25,830
but epidermidis is
differentiated from

254
00:15:25,830 --> 00:15:29,430
aureus in the lab by
the coagulase test,

255
00:15:29,870 --> 00:15:33,290
epidermidis being negative
and aureus positive.

256
00:15:33,450 --> 00:15:36,730
Thus, staphylococcus
epidermidis is frequently

257
00:15:36,730 --> 00:15:40,530
referred to as coagulase
negative staphylococci.

258
00:15:41,050 --> 00:15:43,090
Staphylococci, especially

259
00:15:43,090 --> 00:15:46,670
epidermidis, are
biofilm formers.

260
00:15:46,870 --> 00:15:51,250
Biofilms are virulence
factors. They are thin,

261
00:15:51,310 --> 00:15:54,450
gooey sheets of peptidoglycans
that the bacteria

262
00:15:54,450 --> 00:15:57,710
produce that harbor or
protect the infection

263
00:15:57,710 --> 00:16:01,330
from immune cells,
platelets, and antibiotics.

264
00:16:01,790 --> 00:16:05,770
Biofilms adhere strongly
to surfaces such as bone,

265
00:16:05,770 --> 00:16:09,090
but especially to foreign
bodies such as the

266
00:16:09,090 --> 00:16:12,450
plastic of intravenous or
urinary catheters or the

267
00:16:12,450 --> 00:16:16,610
metal of orthopedic
devices such as knee or hip

268
00:16:16,610 --> 00:16:20,690
replacements or spinal
stabilization screws. And

269
00:16:20,690 --> 00:16:23,790
once a biofilm has formed,
these foreign bodies

270
00:16:23,790 --> 00:16:27,470
usually need to be removed
to cure the infection.

271
00:16:30,650 --> 00:16:33,770
The next group of bacteria
causing nosocomial

272
00:16:33,770 --> 00:16:35,450
infections we'll
talk about are the

273
00:16:35,450 --> 00:16:39,610
enterococci. They are
included as group D in the

274
00:16:39,610 --> 00:16:42,410
Lancefield classification
that is usually

275
00:16:42,410 --> 00:16:45,770
reserved for Streptococci
because until 1984,

276
00:16:45,930 --> 00:16:49,210
the species Fecalis
and Fecium were

277
00:16:49,210 --> 00:16:52,090
classified as Streptococci.
In other words,

278
00:16:52,250 --> 00:16:55,770
Streptococcus Fecalis
and Streptococcus Fecium.

279
00:16:56,050 --> 00:16:58,590
After new analyses, they were

280
00:16:58,590 --> 00:17:00,670
reclassified in
their own genus,

281
00:17:00,810 --> 00:17:01,910
Enterococcus.

282
00:17:02,560 --> 00:17:06,070
Enterococci are commensal
of the GI tract and

283
00:17:06,070 --> 00:17:10,270
transmitted by direct
contact and also on fomites.

284
00:17:10,710 --> 00:17:13,310
With the exception of
urinary tract infections,

285
00:17:13,310 --> 00:17:16,890
infections with these
organisms usually reflect a

286
00:17:16,890 --> 00:17:20,390
breached or suppressed
immune system. For example,

287
00:17:20,730 --> 00:17:22,330
bloodstream
infections in patients

288
00:17:22,330 --> 00:17:23,930
with central venous catheters.

289
00:17:24,090 --> 00:17:27,130
The treatment of
choice for Enterococcus

290
00:17:27,130 --> 00:17:30,970
faecalis is ampicillin.
But the Enterococci are

291
00:17:30,970 --> 00:17:33,830
resistant to some beta
-lactam antibiotics,

292
00:17:33,830 --> 00:17:37,350
specifically the
cephalosporins, treatment

293
00:17:37,350 --> 00:17:39,570
with which can
actually increase your

294
00:17:39,570 --> 00:17:42,390
risk of becoming infected
with Enterococci.

295
00:17:42,650 --> 00:17:45,270
The exception to
this rule would

296
00:17:45,270 --> 00:17:47,390
be the two new cephalosporins,

297
00:17:47,990 --> 00:17:51,550
ceftodiprol and ceftaroline,

298
00:17:51,550 --> 00:17:55,870
which have activity against
Enterococcus fecalis, but

299
00:17:55,870 --> 00:17:59,530
are much less effective
against Enterococcus faecium.

300
00:18:00,190 --> 00:18:03,210
Enterococcus faecium
has become increasingly

301
00:18:03,210 --> 00:18:06,930
resistant to the antibiotic
vancomycin, which greatly

302
00:18:06,930 --> 00:18:09,730
reduces the treatment
options for this infection.

303
00:18:09,730 --> 00:18:12,850
It also requires that
the patient be placed in

304
00:18:12,850 --> 00:18:17,090
contact isolation to prevent
the spread of vancomycin

305
00:18:17,090 --> 00:18:20,730
-resistant enterococcus
faecium to other patients.

306
00:18:24,510 --> 00:18:27,470
So how do you identify
enterococci in the

307
00:18:27,470 --> 00:18:30,270
lab? First, on gram
stain, you see that

308
00:18:30,270 --> 00:18:32,930
you have a gram-positive
caucus in chains.

309
00:18:33,510 --> 00:18:36,450
Biochemical analysis
shows that like

310
00:18:36,450 --> 00:18:38,970
streptococci, but as opposed to

311
00:18:38,970 --> 00:18:42,070
staphylococci, they
are catalase negative.

312
00:18:42,600 --> 00:18:45,570
As shown in the bottom
right tubes on this slide,

313
00:18:45,910 --> 00:18:49,930
enterococci hydrolyzed
bile escalon so that the

314
00:18:49,930 --> 00:18:53,270
bile pigment turns the
media from yellow to black.

315
00:18:54,330 --> 00:18:57,910
Enterococci grow in the
presence of sodium and

316
00:18:57,910 --> 00:19:00,370
bile salts at a broad
range of temperatures.

317
00:19:00,370 --> 00:19:04,290
which is in contrast
to many other bacteria.

318
00:19:09,910 --> 00:19:13,800
Enterococcus faecium is a
common cause of nosocomial

319
00:19:13,800 --> 00:19:17,630
infections. When compared
to Enterococcus faecalis,

320
00:19:17,890 --> 00:19:21,350
Enterococcus faecium is
associated with greater

321
00:19:21,350 --> 00:19:24,710
antimicrobial resistance
so that in addition

322
00:19:24,710 --> 00:19:27,990
to cephalosporins and
vancomycin, it may be

323
00:19:27,990 --> 00:19:30,630
resistant to other
antibiotic classes as well.

324
00:19:30,890 --> 00:19:34,210
When an antibiotic such
as ampicillin results

325
00:19:34,210 --> 00:19:37,470
in bacterial killing, we
say that the antibiotic

326
00:19:37,470 --> 00:19:41,160
is sidal against that
bacteria. That's CIDAL.

327
00:19:42,160 --> 00:19:46,050
This is not the case with
enterococcus and ampicillin.

328
00:19:46,050 --> 00:19:50,100
It is not sidal, but
static. In other words, it

329
00:19:50,100 --> 00:19:53,950
inhibits the growth of the
enterococci, and that action

330
00:19:53,950 --> 00:19:58,350
combined with the host's
immune system results in cure.

331
00:19:58,490 --> 00:20:02,010
This makes some infections
caused by enterococci

332
00:20:02,010 --> 00:20:05,990
to require prolonged
antibiotic courses and

333
00:20:05,990 --> 00:20:09,290
increases the failure rate
of therapy of infection.

334
00:20:13,730 --> 00:20:16,950
The previous bacteria we
covered were gram-positive.

335
00:20:16,950 --> 00:20:19,130
Let's start talking about
some gram-negatives.

336
00:20:19,850 --> 00:20:22,990
Escherichia coli is a
commensal facultative

337
00:20:22,990 --> 00:20:26,890
aerobic gram-negative rod
that colonizes the mammalian

338
00:20:26,890 --> 00:20:29,650
colon. It is the most
common cause of urinary

339
00:20:29,650 --> 00:20:32,510
tract infections and is
a cause of bloodstream

340
00:20:32,510 --> 00:20:35,810
infection and pneumonia
in compromised hosts

341
00:20:35,810 --> 00:20:39,690
and meningitis in neonates
and wound infections,

342
00:20:39,810 --> 00:20:43,750
especially in diabetic
patients. It ferments

343
00:20:43,750 --> 00:20:46,470
lactose causing a greenish
metallic sheen when

344
00:20:46,470 --> 00:20:49,890
grown on eosin-methylene
blue agar as shown on

345
00:20:49,890 --> 00:20:52,230
the plate at the bottom
right of this slide.

346
00:20:52,270 --> 00:20:55,450
When grown in media
containing other indicators,

347
00:20:55,450 --> 00:20:58,410
E. coli is indole
positive and urease

348
00:20:58,410 --> 00:21:02,870
negative. It is typed
using the O, H, and K

349
00:21:02,870 --> 00:21:06,510
antigens that represent
lipopolysaccharide,

350
00:21:06,510 --> 00:21:09,470
flagellum, and capsular
antigens, respectively.

351
00:21:12,750 --> 00:21:16,390
When grown on McConkie
algar, the fermentation

352
00:21:16,390 --> 00:21:20,390
of lactose results in
pink to red colonies.

353
00:21:20,390 --> 00:21:24,670
Growth of E. coli doesn't
result in that metallic

354
00:21:24,670 --> 00:21:28,510
sheen on McConkie, so other
chemical tests need to

355
00:21:28,510 --> 00:21:31,010
differentiate it from other
lactose fermenting gram

356
00:21:31,010 --> 00:21:34,510
-negative rods such as
Klebsiella and Enterobacter.

357
00:21:38,180 --> 00:21:40,750
The next group of gram
-negatives we'll talk about

358
00:21:40,750 --> 00:21:45,490
is Pseudomonas. It is a
motile aerobic non-fermenting

359
00:21:45,490 --> 00:21:48,930
oxidase positive gram
-negative rod. It is found

360
00:21:48,930 --> 00:21:52,630
in soil, humid environments,
and tap water. The most

361
00:21:52,630 --> 00:21:55,470
common species causing
nosocomial infection is

362
00:21:55,470 --> 00:21:59,770
Pseudomonas aruginosa. When
cultured, it produces a

363
00:21:59,770 --> 00:22:03,030
blue-green polycyanin
pigment that you can see here

364
00:22:03,030 --> 00:22:06,230
in the top picture. Its
growth is associated with a

365
00:22:06,230 --> 00:22:10,870
fruity or an odor that is
similar to old sneakers.

366
00:22:11,390 --> 00:22:14,430
It is very commonly
resistant to antibiotics

367
00:22:14,430 --> 00:22:16,810
and has the tendency
to become resistant

368
00:22:16,810 --> 00:22:18,550
to antibiotics to
which it is initially

369
00:22:18,550 --> 00:22:22,150
susceptible in about 10
% of treatment courses.

370
00:22:25,170 --> 00:22:29,490
it more commonly infects
immunocompromised hosts such

371
00:22:29,490 --> 00:22:33,710
as those with agranulocytopenia
or diabetes it causes

372
00:22:33,710 --> 00:22:36,270
chronic colonization and
recurrent pneumonia in

373
00:22:36,270 --> 00:22:39,810
persons with structural lung
disease for example cystic

374
00:22:39,810 --> 00:22:43,050
fibrosis burn victims
are at increased risk of

375
00:22:43,050 --> 00:22:46,530
pseudomonas wound infection
and the isolate can result

376
00:22:46,530 --> 00:22:50,070
in outbreaks in burn units
the picture on the bottom

377
00:22:50,070 --> 00:22:54,390
left is malignant and autitis
externa, a life-threatening

378
00:22:54,390 --> 00:22:57,730
pseudomonas infection
that may require surgical

379
00:22:57,730 --> 00:23:01,330
intervention that occurs
in patients with diabetes.

380
00:23:04,830 --> 00:23:07,910
Another gram-negative
rod is Klebsiella.

381
00:23:07,910 --> 00:23:11,010
It is a lactose
-fermenting ureacin

382
00:23:11,010 --> 00:23:13,710
-indole-positive
gram-negative rod.

383
00:23:13,790 --> 00:23:17,910
Several species cause infection,
most commonly Klebsiella

384
00:23:17,910 --> 00:23:22,770
pneumoniae, and based on
typing, on the capsular

385
00:23:22,770 --> 00:23:28,450
antigen, specifically the
capsular lipopolysaccharide,

386
00:23:28,450 --> 00:23:32,910
or O antigen, and the
capsular polysaccharide, or K

387
00:23:32,910 --> 00:23:37,610
antigen. It produces a
capsular polysaccharide that

388
00:23:37,610 --> 00:23:42,050
is extremely thick, resulting
in these bacteria appearing

389
00:23:42,050 --> 00:23:47,130
very large on gram stain,
almost like boxcars, and

390
00:23:47,130 --> 00:23:52,250
mucoid colonies when grown
on auger plates. This mucoid

391
00:23:52,250 --> 00:23:56,670
gel, like biofilms, is
a virulence factor that

392
00:23:56,670 --> 00:24:00,530
protects the bacteria from
immune cells and antibiotics.

393
00:24:04,730 --> 00:24:07,350
When you touch colonies
of Klebsiella with

394
00:24:07,350 --> 00:24:11,030
a loop, the mucousy
colony will adhere and

395
00:24:11,030 --> 00:24:13,870
form a string from
the plate to the loop.

396
00:24:14,170 --> 00:24:17,090
In purulent infections
such as pneumonia,

397
00:24:17,350 --> 00:24:21,750
that mucoid capsular
polysaccharide produces

398
00:24:21,750 --> 00:24:25,450
pus that is like
jelly and very heavy.

399
00:24:25,570 --> 00:24:28,690
Here is an x-ray
showing almost complete

400
00:24:28,690 --> 00:24:31,510
opacification of the
left lower lobe because

401
00:24:31,510 --> 00:24:34,470
the lobe is filled
with this mucousy pus.

402
00:24:35,790 --> 00:24:38,930
If the infection occurs
just over the right middle

403
00:24:38,930 --> 00:24:42,410
lobe fissure, the weight
of the mucus can cause

404
00:24:42,410 --> 00:24:47,070
the misshapen middle lobe
fissure to form an s shape

405
00:24:47,070 --> 00:24:51,130
the so-called s sign and
that is suggestive of

406
00:24:51,130 --> 00:24:59,970
klebsiella pneumonia once
pneumonia is well established

407
00:24:59,970 --> 00:25:03,450
within a lobe tissue
destruction may result

408
00:25:03,450 --> 00:25:08,550
in cavitation and abscess
formation or it may spread

409
00:25:08,550 --> 00:25:12,970
to the pleural space,
causing a thick empyema.

410
00:25:16,510 --> 00:25:21,190
Klebsiella can also cause
many other infections other

411
00:25:21,190 --> 00:25:24,950
than pneumonia, including
nosocomial bloodstream

412
00:25:24,950 --> 00:25:29,390
infections and urinary tract
infections and wound infections.

413
00:25:29,410 --> 00:25:33,470
Risk factors for nosocomial
Klebsiella infection

414
00:25:33,470 --> 00:25:37,270
include central venous or
urinary catheter placement,

415
00:25:37,430 --> 00:25:39,550
mechanical ventilation, and

416
00:25:39,550 --> 00:25:41,350
antibiotic administration.

417
00:25:43,750 --> 00:25:47,150
Klebsiella oxytocca
has also been

418
00:25:47,150 --> 00:25:50,110
identified in outbreaks
of premature newborns

419
00:25:50,110 --> 00:25:52,730
in neonatal
intensive care units.

420
00:25:58,370 --> 00:26:02,590
Acinetobacter is an
aerobic non-fermenting gram

421
00:26:02,590 --> 00:26:06,130
-negative coxobacillus
that is oxidase negative.

422
00:26:06,130 --> 00:26:09,630
It is an important
opportunistic pathogen that

423
00:26:09,630 --> 00:26:12,690
usually does not cause
infection in normal hosts.

424
00:26:12,850 --> 00:26:15,930
It has the ability to grow in

425
00:26:15,930 --> 00:26:17,770
many different environments.

426
00:26:17,810 --> 00:26:19,790
It forms biofilms.

427
00:26:20,070 --> 00:26:24,270
In the mid-1990s, it
became a prominent cause

428
00:26:24,270 --> 00:26:26,470
of outbreaks of
wounds and bloodstream

429
00:26:26,470 --> 00:26:30,090
infections in post-surgical
intensive care units.

430
00:26:30,330 --> 00:26:33,850
These isolates were
frequently resistant to many

431
00:26:33,850 --> 00:26:37,570
antibiotics, and this bacteria
has virulence mechanisms

432
00:26:37,570 --> 00:26:41,470
that allow it to become resistant
to many of the antibiotics

433
00:26:41,470 --> 00:26:44,330
that were still available
for treatment so that

434
00:26:44,330 --> 00:26:46,870
it was not uncommon for
these isolates to become

435
00:26:46,870 --> 00:26:51,830
increasingly and finally pan
-resistant to all antibiotics.

436
00:26:52,070 --> 00:26:55,850
These multi-drug resistant
isolates later became a

437
00:26:55,850 --> 00:27:00,010
common cause of wound infection
in returning veterans.

438
00:27:04,450 --> 00:27:09,030
The biofilm it produces
makes it a potent nosocomial

439
00:27:09,030 --> 00:27:11,810
pathogen resulting in
bloodstream infections

440
00:27:11,810 --> 00:27:15,090
from infected venous
catheters, urinary tract

441
00:27:15,090 --> 00:27:18,590
infections from urinary
catheters, and surgical

442
00:27:18,590 --> 00:27:22,070
site infections from
infected Penrose drains.

443
00:27:23,050 --> 00:27:26,050
acinetobacter can live on
surfaces for up to five

444
00:27:26,050 --> 00:27:30,610
months and is difficult to
eradicate with the normal

445
00:27:30,610 --> 00:27:34,630
hospital disinfectants that
are used to clean rooms once

446
00:27:39,390 --> 00:27:42,430
the infection establishes
in a wound or in the lungs

447
00:27:42,430 --> 00:27:45,570
it can disseminate
resulting in bloodstream and

448
00:27:45,570 --> 00:27:48,950
metastatic infection of the
meninges and practically

449
00:27:48,950 --> 00:27:52,690
any other organ
acinetobacter has also been

450
00:27:52,690 --> 00:27:57,590
implicated in nosocomial
necrotizing fasciitis, which

451
00:27:57,590 --> 00:28:00,310
is shown in the bottom
right picture on this slide.

452
00:28:05,460 --> 00:28:07,360
And finally, we're going to

453
00:28:07,360 --> 00:28:09,080
talk about candida species.

454
00:28:09,640 --> 00:28:14,120
These cause oral or
****** candidiasis as well

455
00:28:14,120 --> 00:28:17,800
as candidal dermatitis,
but they are also the

456
00:28:17,800 --> 00:28:21,580
third most common cause
of nosocomial bloodstream

457
00:28:21,580 --> 00:28:25,020
infections, usually in
patients with certain

458
00:28:25,020 --> 00:28:28,240
risk factors, such as
after abdominal surgery,

459
00:28:28,460 --> 00:28:30,800
central venous
catheter placement,

460
00:28:31,000 --> 00:28:34,920
broad-spectrum antimicrobacterial
antibiotics, and

461
00:28:34,920 --> 00:28:39,480
steroid therapy, as well
as parenteral nutrition. As

462
00:28:39,480 --> 00:28:41,880
you can imagine, there are
patients in the intensive

463
00:28:41,880 --> 00:28:45,280
care unit with all of these
risk factors, and some

464
00:28:45,280 --> 00:28:48,980
other risk factors are also
listed here on this slide.

465
00:28:53,770 --> 00:28:57,950
Although Candida albicans is
the most commonly isolated

466
00:28:57,950 --> 00:29:02,170
candida species, due to
the overuse of fluconazole,

467
00:29:02,610 --> 00:29:06,390
antifungal resistant candidal
strains have increased

468
00:29:06,390 --> 00:29:10,870
in prevalence. And as you can
imagine, these make treating

469
00:29:10,870 --> 00:29:14,730
candida infection more
complicated and expensive.

470
00:29:15,190 --> 00:29:19,270
Many isolates of candida
glabrata have reduced

471
00:29:19,270 --> 00:29:23,190
susceptibility or are
intrinsically resistant

472
00:29:23,190 --> 00:29:26,030
to fluconazole.
They were once very

473
00:29:26,030 --> 00:29:30,750
uncommon, but now make
up about 25% of Candida

474
00:29:30,750 --> 00:29:34,170
species recovered from
nosocomial infections.

475
00:29:34,410 --> 00:29:38,470
A recently isolated,
broadly resistant Candida

476
00:29:38,470 --> 00:29:42,090
species, Candida auris,
was first isolated in

477
00:29:42,090 --> 00:29:44,630
Europe and has since
spread to hospitals in the

478
00:29:44,630 --> 00:29:47,570
United States and the
rest of the world. Now

479
00:29:52,960 --> 00:29:56,640
let's look specifically at
one nosocomial infection,

480
00:29:56,900 --> 00:30:01,240
nosocomial pneumonia or
hospital acquired pneumonia.

481
00:30:01,300 --> 00:30:04,200
Again, this is an
infection that occurs

482
00:30:04,200 --> 00:30:06,680
during the hospitalization
and it is not

483
00:30:06,680 --> 00:30:09,040
present upon admission
to the hospital.

484
00:30:09,360 --> 00:30:13,480
Nosocomial pneumonia is a
severe and possibly life

485
00:30:13,480 --> 00:30:16,100
-threatening infection that
results in insignificant

486
00:30:16,100 --> 00:30:20,760
morbidity and possible
mortality for the patient. In

487
00:30:20,760 --> 00:30:24,300
addition, it increases the
cost of hospital stay, both

488
00:30:24,300 --> 00:30:28,020
because of prolonged
hospitalization and the need for

489
00:30:28,020 --> 00:30:31,500
prolonged antibiotic therapy,
usually broad spectrum,

490
00:30:31,500 --> 00:30:34,680
and possible admission to
the intensive care unit.

491
00:30:34,680 --> 00:30:37,420
As we'll see later,
the intensive care

492
00:30:37,420 --> 00:30:40,480
unit is the hospital
location where nosocomial

493
00:30:40,480 --> 00:30:42,920
pneumonia is most
likely to occur.

494
00:30:44,880 --> 00:30:47,620
Pneumonia generally
occurs by inhalation of an

495
00:30:47,620 --> 00:30:50,300
organism that colonizes
the respiratory tract,

496
00:30:50,300 --> 00:30:53,820
later causing lower
respiratory tract infection.

497
00:30:53,820 --> 00:30:57,120
It can also occur by
aspiration of gastric

498
00:30:57,120 --> 00:31:00,280
contents, for example,
after syncope or seizure.

499
00:31:01,180 --> 00:31:03,160
Hematogenously
acquired pneumonia is

500
00:31:03,160 --> 00:31:06,320
less common, but
can happen when the

501
00:31:06,320 --> 00:31:08,760
root of infection
is not the lungs.

502
00:31:11,920 --> 00:31:14,660
Hundreds of hospitals
throughout the United States

503
00:31:14,660 --> 00:31:17,340
report infection data to
their Centers for Disease

504
00:31:17,340 --> 00:31:21,040
Control through the National
Healthcare Safety Network.

505
00:31:21,300 --> 00:31:24,220
This data has shown that
nosocomial pneumonia

506
00:31:24,220 --> 00:31:27,480
accounts for about
31% of all ICU

507
00:31:27,480 --> 00:31:31,480
infections, the mortality
of which reaches 50%.

508
00:31:31,860 --> 00:31:34,460
Because the intensive
care unit is the ward in

509
00:31:34,460 --> 00:31:37,140
the hospital that uses the
most antibiotics, patients

510
00:31:37,220 --> 00:31:40,900
often become colonized
with multi-drug resistant

511
00:31:40,900 --> 00:31:44,080
bacteria such as
Klebsiella, Pseudomonas

512
00:31:44,080 --> 00:31:48,100
aeruginosa, Acinetobacter,
and Staphylococcus aureus.

513
00:31:48,340 --> 00:31:51,340
Outbreaks of legionellosis
have also occurred

514
00:31:51,340 --> 00:31:53,900
in hospitals, but
these are less common.

515
00:31:56,640 --> 00:32:00,600
The criteria for the diagnosis
of nosocomial pneumonia

516
00:32:00,600 --> 00:32:05,020
is modified every few years
as new data emerges. But

517
00:32:05,020 --> 00:32:08,920
in general, when a patient
in the ICU has a new fever,

518
00:32:08,920 --> 00:32:13,020
increasing oxygen requirements,
increasing respiratory

519
00:32:13,020 --> 00:32:17,300
secretions, and a new
opacity on chest x-ray, the

520
00:32:17,300 --> 00:32:21,300
diagnosis of nosocomial
pneumonia is made. If the patient

521
00:32:21,300 --> 00:32:23,940
is being mechanically
ventilated, the further

522
00:32:23,940 --> 00:32:28,130
subdefinition of ventilator
-associated pneumonia is made.

523
00:32:28,130 --> 00:32:32,180
Immediately after obtaining
a microbiologic specimen,

524
00:32:32,840 --> 00:32:36,500
empiric antibiotic therapy
is begun based on the

525
00:32:36,500 --> 00:32:40,100
predominant organisms
causing infections in the

526
00:32:40,100 --> 00:32:45,000
hospital and the ICU and the
antibiotic susceptibility

527
00:32:45,000 --> 00:32:49,380
patterns of those
organisms. In addition,

528
00:32:49,380 --> 00:32:52,060
pulmonary hygiene is very
important for patients

529
00:32:52,060 --> 00:32:55,020
who are mechanically
ventilated and specifically

530
00:32:55,020 --> 00:33:00,020
elevation of the head to
30 to 45 degrees decreases

531
00:33:00,020 --> 00:33:02,840
the risk of aspiration
and nosocomial pneumonia

532
00:33:02,840 --> 00:33:06,520
in those patients on
mechanical ventilation.

533
00:33:06,620 --> 00:33:09,640
These patients are always
frequently suctioned

534
00:33:09,640 --> 00:33:13,040
to maintain clear airways
of respiratory secretions

535
00:33:13,040 --> 00:33:15,920
to decrease the risk of
aspiration around the

536
00:33:15,920 --> 00:33:18,960
endotracheal tube, the
most common root of

537
00:33:18,960 --> 00:33:21,640
infection-causing ventilator
-associated pneumonia.

538
00:33:22,370 --> 00:33:25,480
Previously, the ICU staff
would have to change the

539
00:33:25,480 --> 00:33:28,680
position of the patient every
few hours, but newer beds

540
00:33:28,680 --> 00:33:31,740
in the ICU have mechanisms
to do this automatically.

541
00:33:34,820 --> 00:33:38,820
So in summary, we've defined
the nosocomial infections

542
00:33:38,820 --> 00:33:43,000
and their prevalence, reviewed
the most common nosocomial

543
00:33:43,000 --> 00:33:46,620
etiologic agents, and
used nosocomial pneumonia

544
00:33:46,620 --> 00:33:50,260
as an example. We've reviewed
the importance of infection

545
00:33:50,260 --> 00:33:54,400
control and major causes
of nosocomial infections.

546
00:33:54,400 --> 00:33:57,820
We've reviewed infection
control programs, including

547
00:33:57,820 --> 00:34:01,260
the importance of hand washing,
isolation, and precautions.

548
00:34:01,260 --> 00:34:04,760
We've reviewed appropriate
isolation and precautions

549
00:34:04,760 --> 00:34:08,400
for specific organisms.
And we've reviewed the

550
00:34:08,400 --> 00:34:12,180
challenges nosocomial disease
presents for patient care.

551
00:34:14,320 --> 00:34:22,340
I've listed on this slide
a resource, and I want to

552
00:34:22,340 --> 00:34:25,420
thank you very much for
listening to this lecture.

553
00:34:25,460 --> 00:34:30,940
Please note my office hours
and email address. During

554
00:34:30,940 --> 00:34:34,580
office hours, we will review
this and other lectures.

555
00:34:34,740 --> 00:34:38,460
Thank you so much for
listening, and be well.